# Per-package coverage baseline — a ratchet, not a floor.
#
# `make coverage-check` fails when any package here measures BELOW its recorded
# number, and says which package, by how much, on both sides. AGENTS.md §5 asks
# for exactly that shape: a change to internal/secrets, internal/authz or
# internal/sync that drops their coverage is a failed change, which is a
# statement about a drop and not about an absolute threshold. A single
# repository-wide floor could not express it — it would either sit low enough
# to be meaningless or high enough to block a legitimate refactor, and it
# would say nothing about the three packages the rule names.
#
# A package that RISES prints RAISED and does not fail; run
# `make coverage-baseline` and commit the result to ratchet it up. A package
# with no row here is reported UNTRACKED and does not fail, so a new package
# can land without a gate — but a package that disappears while its row stays
# is a MISSING and does fail, because that is how a test file gets deleted
# without anyone noticing.
#
# Resolution is 0.1 percentage points: both sides are compared after rounding
# to one decimal, which is the precision this file records. Regenerate with
# `make cover && make coverage-baseline`; never hand-edit a number.
#
# Measured 2026-09-30 on commit 9518ed0 with `make cover`, which is
# `./scripts/test.sh -coverprofile=coverage.out -covermode=atomic` run under
# GOTOOLCHAIN=go1.26.6 (total across all packages: 74.2%).
#
# The toolchain is part of the measurement and is recorded at the foot of this
# file as a machine-written line, because a coverage percentage is a property of
# the compiler as well as of the tests. Measured on that same commit, with that
# same command and no source change:
#
#   package            go1.26.6   go1.27.1
#   /internal/config      94.2       94.9
#   /internal/plugin      91.7       92.4
#   /internal/app         72.0       74.3
#   /cmd/semiplane        54.3       50.0
#
# The numbers in this file were previously recorded on go1.27.1 and compared
